"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[634],{3634:s=>{s.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2017-04-26T14:29:00","modified":"2017-04-26T17:25:00","id":1034,"title":"React Router v4 with Hash Fallback","article":"<p>I was developing an SPA (Single Page Application) in React, with React Router v4 and Webpack 2. I needed to support IE9 and Android 4.0 and I wanted to use standard URLs for routing, however these browsers dont support the history API that was needed, so I had to look at my options.</p>\\n<p>From researching I came up with a couple of options; simply use hash based routing for all browsers or attempt to lazy load a drop in replacement for browsers that dont support the history API.</p>\\n<p>If I didnt &#39;need&#39; to use standard URLs I would just use hash based routing as pointed out by <a href=\\"http://jamesknelson.com/push-state-vs-hash-based-routing-with-react-js/\\">James K Nelson</a> its more than suitable. But I choose the more difficult route obviously, I mean thats what developers do right?</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"how-do-we-know-the-browser-needs-a-fallback\\">How do we know the browser needs a fallback?</h2>\\n<p>The first thing to do is check if the browser supports the history API, but as there is a <a href=\\"https://github.com/Modernizr/Modernizr/blob/master/feature-detects/history.js\\">bug in some browsers implementation of the history API</a>, this isnt as simple as checking for the history object. </p>\\n<p>You could use <a href=\\"https://modernizr.com\\">Modernizr</a> to check if <a href=\\"https://github.com/Modernizr/Modernizr/blob/master/feature-detects/history.js\\">history</a> works or as we are using React Router v4 and it uses the <a href=\\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/history\\">history</a> npm package, it makes sense to use that.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { supportsHistory } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'history/DOMUtils\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> requiresHash = !supportsHistory();</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"lazy-loading\\">Lazy Loading</h2>\\n<p>Fortunately with Webpack its quite simple to split the fallback out of the bundle to enable lazy loading. We just need to use webpacks require.ensure method.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>.ensure([<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>], <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span> =&gt; {\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> Router = <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>).default;\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// ... Handle Router</span>\\n});</code></pre>\\n<p>I came up with two ways of using this for my app that are fairly clean, the first is getting the correct router prior to booting the app and the second uses a Component wrapper for the Router that updates when loaded.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"option-1-a-promise-before-first-render\\">Option 1: A Promise before first render</h3>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// getRouter.js</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { supportsHistory } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'history/DOMUtils\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { BrowserRouter } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> requiresHash = !supportsHistory();\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">/**\\n* Returns a promise, thats resolves to either the default BrowserRouter or falls back to a lazy loaded HashRouter if required\\n*/</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">export</span> <span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">getRouter</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>) </span>{\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">Promise</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-params\\">resolve</span> =&gt;</span> {\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">if</span> (requiresHash) {\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>.ensure([<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>], <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span> =&gt;\\n                resolve(<span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>).default)\\n            );\\n        }\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">else</span> {\\n            resolve(BrowserRouter);\\n        }\\n    });\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// app.jsx</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> React <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> ReactDOM <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { Route } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { getRouter } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'./getRouter\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">get</span><span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Router</span>().<span class=\\"hljs-title\\">then</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\">Router =&gt; {\\n\\n    function App(</span>) {\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> (\\n            <span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">basename</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"/\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n                {/* Add routes here */}\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span>&gt;</span></span>\\n        );\\n    }\\n\\n    ReactDOM.render(<span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">App</span> /&gt;</span></span>, <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">document</span>.getElementById(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'app\'</span>));\\n});</code></pre>\\n<p>The advantage of this is it doesnt abstract the fact its is not a normal Router component being used and it is efficient as it doesnt attempt to render anything at all until the main component Router is ready to be used.</p>\\n<p>Although I have used a promise here, you could have just used a callback.</p>\\n<h3 id=\\"option-2-a-wrapped-component\\">Option 2: A wrapped component</h3>\\n<p>Arguably the more &#39;react&#39; way of doing this is to use a component to load the correct Router and only display the Router and its children once its ready.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// Router.js</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> React, { PureComponent } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { supportsHistory } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'history/DOMUtils\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { BrowserRouter } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> requiresHash = !supportsHistory();\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">/**\\n* A Router that by default uses a BrowserRouter, but if not supported falls back and lazy loads HashRouter\\n*/</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">export</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">default</span> <span class=\\"hljs-class\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">class</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">Router</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">extends</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">PureComponent</span> </span>{\\n\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">constructor</span>(props) {\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">super</span>(props);\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">this</span>.state = {\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">Router</span>: requiresHash ? <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">null</span> : BrowserRouter\\n        };\\n    }\\n\\n    componentWillMount() {\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">if</span> (!<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">this</span>.state.Router) {\\n\\n            <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>.ensure([<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>],\\n                <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span> =&gt; <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">this</span>.setState({\\n                    <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">Router</span>: <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">require</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom/HashRouter\'</span>).default\\n                })\\n            );\\n        }\\n    }\\n    render() {\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> { Router } = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">this</span>.state;\\n\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> Router ? <span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span> {<span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">...this.props</span>} /&gt;</span></span> : <span class=\\"hljs-literal\\">null</span>;\\n    }\\n}</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// app.jsx</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> React <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> ReactDOM <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { Route } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> Router <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'./Router\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">App</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>) </span>{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> (\\n        <span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">basename</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"/\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n            {/* Add routes here */}\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span>&gt;</span></span>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nReactDOM.render(<span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">App</span> /&gt;</span></span>, <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">document</span>.getElementById(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'app\'</span>));</code></pre>\\n<p>The advantage to this pattern is it is clean, easy to understand and you can adapt it to show loading icons if desired, as under the hood its just a component.</p>\\n<p>There is a disadvantage though, when the render function of App is run the children of Router will still be evaluated in code, remember that jsx is just converted into React.createElement calls, I have discussed it more in <a href=\\"https://www.lski.uk/blog/1033\\">Compiled React</a> making it less efficient than option 1.</p>\\n<p>In the end I used option one, but its not a clear cut choice.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"handling-the-switch\\">Handling The Switch</h2>\\n<p>Of course the main issue now is when someone goes to a direct link on the SPA that isnt hash based on a browser that is hash based. </p>\\n<p>In my situation the SPA was going to be hosted on github pages, which required some additional hoops to jump through. The premise is the same though, instead of letting the Router create the history object, we create the history object ourselves and pass it into the Router, using it to redirect to the hashed version of the current route.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// history.js</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { createBrowserHistory } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'history\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { supportsHistory } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'history/DOMUtils\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> requiresHash = !supportsHistory();\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">const</span> history = createBrowserHistory();\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">if</span>(requiresHash &amp;&amp; !history.location.hash) {\\n    history.push(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'/#\'</span> + history.location.pathname);\\n}\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">export</span> <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">default</span> history;</code></pre>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-js\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// app.jsx</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> React <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> ReactDOM <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> { Route } <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'react-router-dom\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> Router <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'./Router\'</span>;\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">import</span> history <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">from</span> <span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'./history\'</span>;\\n\\n<span class=\\"hljs-function\\"><span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">function</span> <span class=\\"hljs-title\\">App</span>(<span class=\\"hljs-params\\"></span>) </span>{\\n    <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">return</span> (\\n        <span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">history</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">{history}</span> <span class=\\"hljs-attr\\">basename</span>=<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"/\\"</span>&gt;</span>\\n            {/* Add routes here */}\\n        <span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;/<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">Router</span>&gt;</span></span>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nReactDOM.render(<span class=\\"xml\\"><span class=\\"hljs-tag\\">&lt;<span class=\\"hljs-name\\">App</span> /&gt;</span></span>, <span class=\\"hljs-built_in\\">document</span>.getElementById(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\'app\'</span>));</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"issues\\">Issues</h2>\\n<p>You need to be aware there is a limitation to using the fallback approach here. When developing you always need to program to your lowest denominator, which in this case is the HashRouter. This means you cant use some of the more advanced features of BrowserRouter such as handling of pushState etc.</p>\\n<p>This does make the hash only routing seem more attractive as an option.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["react","javascript","webpack"]}')}}]);